\section{Setting up the R-SPQ-2F surveys, literately}
\label{app:rspq}

This appendix is the paper's third literate program.
It implements the two administrations of the R-SPQ-2F
(\cref{approach-measure,end-measure}) as Canvas surveys, and their
harvest.
Two programs are tangled from this section: [[make_rspq.py]], which
writes the survey descriptions [[rspq-start.json]] and
[[rspq-end.json]] that the [[canvaslms]] tool pushes to Canvas, and
[[analyze_rspq.py]], which fetches both administrations' reports back
from Canvas and writes [[rspq.csv]] for the episode analysis
(\cref{app:episodes}).

Unlike the quizzes of \cref{app:quiz}, the surveys are not written as
JSON directly: the twenty items share one form---the same five
responses, the same settings---so the instrument lives here once, as
constants, and [[make_rspq.py]] unfolds it into the JSON that the
platform tool consumes:
\begin{minted}{bash}
python3 make_rspq.py
canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f rspq-start.json
canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f rspq-end.json
\end{minted}
Both programs include the same constants chunk below, so the surveys
and their analysis cannot drift apart.
Consent is not asked again here: it is collected once, at the head of
the background quiz (\cref{app:quiz}, \cref{ethics}).

\subsection{The instrument as constants}

The survey titles are the keys by which the analysis finds the two
administrations in Canvas, and they name the difference between them:
the start administration asks about the student's studying in general,
the end administration about this course (\cref{end-measure}).
The item titles are zero-padded ([[Item 01]]) so that they sort in
item order wherever Canvas or a spreadsheet lists them; the report's
columns are re-keyed by these titles before [[column_for]] looks them
up (\cref{app:reading-the-report}), so nothing else depends on their
form.
The file names are constants for the same reason the titles are: the
program that writes the descriptions and the program that reads the
answers back must agree on them, and one definition cannot disagree
with itself.

<<the instrument as constants>>=
SURVEY_TITLES = {
    "start": "Study process questionnaire: your studying in general",
    "end": "Study process questionnaire: your studying in this course",
}
ITEM_TITLES = [f"Item {number:02d}" for number in range(1, 21)]
SURVEY_FILES = {which: f"rspq-{which}.json" for which in SURVEY_TITLES}
@

The descriptions also place the surveys in the course's modules, as
the quizzes of \cref{app:quiz} do: a [[modules]] entry names the
module by a regular expression, the position in it, and what completes
the item.
The start survey follows the background quiz and the debugging quiz at
the end of the module that introduces the Python part, which every
later Python module requires; the end survey follows the end-of-course
debugging quiz in the module that closes the course.
The students must submit the survey to complete the module, as with
the quizzes.

<<the instrument as constants>>=
MODULES = {
    "start": [{"module": "^Översikt Python-delen$", "position": 7,
               "completion_requirement": "must_submit"}],
    "end": [{"module": "^Avslutande diagnostiskt prov$", "position": 3,
             "completion_requirement": "must_submit"}],
}
@

The items and the five responses are those of the questionnaire as
printed in the appendix of \textcite{RSPQ2F}, verbatim
(\cref{approach-measure}); choosing response \(k\) scores \(k\) on the
five-point scale that the episode analysis sums
(\cref{app:episodes}).

<<the instrument as constants>>=
RESPONSES = [
    "this item is never or only rarely true of me",
    "this item is sometimes true of me",
    "this item is true of me about half the time",
    "this item is frequently true of me",
    "this item is always or almost always true of me",
]
ITEMS = [
    "I find that at times studying gives me a feeling of deep personal satisfaction.",
    "I find that I have to do enough work on a topic so that I can form my own conclusions before I am satisfied.",
    "My aim is to pass the course while doing as little work as possible.",
    "I only study seriously what's given out in class or in the course outlines.",
    "I feel that virtually any topic can be highly interesting once I get into it.",
    "I find most new topics interesting and often spend extra time trying to obtain more information about them.",
    "I do not find my course very interesting so I keep my work to the minimum.",
    "I learn some things by rote, going over and over them until I know them by heart even if I do not understand them.",
    "I find that studying academic topics can at times be as exciting as a good novel or movie.",
    "I test myself on important topics until I understand them completely.",
    "I find I can get by in most assessments by memorising key sections rather than trying to understand them.",
    "I generally restrict my study to what is specifically set as I think it is unnecessary to do anything extra.",
    "I work hard at my studies because I find the material interesting.",
    "I spend a lot of my free time finding out more about interesting topics which have been discussed in different classes.",
    "I find it is not helpful to study topics in depth. It confuses and wastes time, when all you need is a passing acquaintance with topics.",
    "I believe that lecturers shouldn't expect students to spend significant amounts of time studying material everyone knows won't be examined.",
    "I come to most classes with questions in mind that I want answering.",
    "I make a point of looking at most of the suggested readings that go with the lectures.",
    "I see no point in learning material which is not likely to be in the examination.",
    "I find the best way to pass examinations is to try to remember answers to likely questions.",
]
@

The instructions are the only part that differs between the two
administrations (\cref{end-measure}).
The start survey keeps \citeauthor{RSPQ2F}'s original preliminary
instructions with one adaptation: the two sentences about the paper
answer sheet (\enquote{Please fill in the appropriate circle
alongside the question number on the `General Purpose Survey/Answer
Sheet'\ldots}) are replaced by a sentence noting that every question
offers the same five responses, since Canvas shows them with each
item.
The end survey's instructions instead ask about this course---the
adaptation \textcite{RSPQ2F} themselves recommend for course-level
use, quoted in \cref{end-measure}: the opening sentences name this
course rather than the studies in general, and the sentence about
choosing the most important subject is dropped, since the subject is
now fixed.
The rest is verbatim in both.

<<the instrument as constants>>=
COMMON_INSTRUCTIONS = (
    "<p>Each question offers the same five responses."
    " Please choose the one most appropriate response to each"
    " question: the one that best fits your immediate reaction."
    " Do not spend a long time on each item; your first reaction is"
    " probably the best one. Please answer each item.</p>"
    "<p>Do not worry about projecting a good image."
    " Your answers are confidential."
    " Thank you for your cooperation.</p>"
)
INSTRUCTIONS = {
    "start": (
        "<p>This questionnaire has a number of questions about your"
        " attitudes towards your studies and your usual way of"
        " studying. There is no right way of studying. It depends on"
        " what suits your own style and the course you are studying."
        " It is accordingly important that you answer each question"
        " as honestly as you can. If you think your answer to a"
        " question would depend on the subject being studied, give"
        " the answer that would apply to the subject(s) most"
        " important to you.</p>" + COMMON_INSTRUCTIONS
    ),
    "end": (
        "<p>This questionnaire has a number of questions about your"
        " attitudes towards this course and your way of studying in"
        " it. There is no right way of studying. It depends on what"
        " suits your own style and the course. It is accordingly"
        " important that you answer each question as honestly as you"
        " can, with this course in mind.</p>" + COMMON_INSTRUCTIONS
    ),
}
@

\subsection{Writing the survey descriptions}

The survey settings mirror those of the quizzes in \cref{app:quiz}:
no time limit, a single attempt, no shuffling---the R-SPQ-2F's item
order is part of the validated instrument---and, since there is
nothing to grade, the result view of the background quiz, which
reveals neither points nor correctness.

<<[[make_rspq.py]]>>=
"""Write the R-SPQ-2F survey descriptions for Canvas.

The two files, rspq-start.json and rspq-end.json, are pushed to the
learning platform with canvaslms; the surveys differ only in their
title and instructions.  Tangled from the appendix of the paper,
which documents the instrument and the adaptations.
"""

import json

<<the instrument as constants>>
<<build one survey description>>
<<write both survey files>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
@

Canvas requires a \enquote{correct} answer even in an ungraded
survey.
The [[scoring_data]] value is arbitrary and never shown, as in
\cref{app:quiz}.
The quiz settings follow the ungraded quiz of \cref{app:quiz} too:
the attempts and result-view keys sit in the [[multiple_attempts]]
and [[result_view_settings]] objects, because Canvas ignores them when
written flat, and the result view hides correctness and points so
that the survey reads as a survey.

<<build one survey description>>=
def item(number):
    """Return the survey item for the 1-based item number."""
    return {
        "position": number,
        "points_possible": 0,
        "entry": {
            "title": ITEM_TITLES[number - 1],
            "item_body": f"<p>{ITEMS[number - 1]}</p>",
            "interaction_type_slug": "choice",
            "interaction_data": {
                "choices": [
                    {"position": value, "item_body": f"<p>{response}</p>"}
                    for value, response in enumerate(RESPONSES, start=1)
                ]
            },
            "scoring_data": {"value": 1},
            "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence",
        },
    }


def survey(which):
    """Return the survey description for the start or end administration."""
    return {
        "quiz_type": "new",
        "settings": {
            "title": SURVEY_TITLES[which],
            "instructions": INSTRUCTIONS[which],
            "points_possible": 0,
            "modules": MODULES[which],
            "time_limit": None,
            "due_at": None,
            "unlock_at": None,
            "lock_at": None,
            "quiz_settings": {
                "shuffle_answers": False,
                "shuffle_questions": False,
                "has_time_limit": False,
                "one_at_a_time_type": "none",
                "allow_backtracking": True,
                "multiple_attempts": {"multiple_attempts_enabled": False},
                "result_view_settings": {
                    "result_view_restricted": True,
                    "display_items": True,
                    "display_item_response": True,
                    "display_item_response_correctness": False,
                    "display_item_correct_answer": False,
                    "display_points_awarded": False,
                    "display_points_possible": False,
                },
            },
        },
        "items": [item(number) for number in range(1, 21)],
    }
@

<<write both survey files>>=
def main():
    for which in SURVEY_TITLES:
        filename = SURVEY_FILES[which]
        with open(filename, "w") as outfile:
            json.dump(survey(which), outfile, indent=2)
        print(f"wrote {filename}")
@

\subsection{Fetching the answers}

The results come back the same way the quiz results do: the fetcher
of \cref{app:quiz-analysis} takes a quiz title and the item titles of
that quiz, so this program imports it and passes the survey titles
together with the titles read from the survey descriptions next to the
program---the report names its columns by the item bodies, not by the
titles (\cref{app:reading-the-report}), and the description is what
maps the one to the other.
Each report row is one student; the item columns hold the chosen
response texts, which map back to the five-point scale by their
position in [[RESPONSES]].
A student who did not answer every item cannot be scored---the
approach scores sum all twenty items (\cref{app:episodes})---so
incomplete rows are reported and skipped, not written with holes.

<<[[analyze_rspq.py]]>>=
"""Fetch the R-SPQ-2F survey results and write rspq.csv.

The surveys are described by rspq-start.json and rspq-end.json,
written by make_rspq.py; this program and those files are tangled
from the same literate source (the appendix of the paper), so the
titles and response texts cannot drift apart.  The results come
straight from Canvas via the fetcher of analyze_quiz (appendix A).

Requires canvaslms (and its configured credentials).
"""

import argparse
import csv
import os
import sys

import analyze_quiz

<<the instrument as constants>>
<<map a report row to the twenty answers>>
<<write the rspq csv>>
<<rspq main>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
@

<<map a report row to the twenty answers>>=
def answers(report_row):
    """Return the item1..item20 dict for one report row, or None."""
    result = {}
    for number, title in enumerate(ITEM_TITLES, start=1):
        column = analyze_quiz.column_for(title, report_row.keys())
        cell = report_row.get(column, "") if column else ""
        value = next(
            (
                value
                for value, response in enumerate(RESPONSES, start=1)
                if response in cell
            ),
            None,
        )
        if value is None:
            return None
        result[f"item{number}"] = value
    return result
@

The output follows the [[rspq.csv]] schema of \cref{app:episodes}:
one row per student and administration, with [[student]] keyed the
same way as in the quiz analysis---by [[analyze_quiz.student_of]],
that is by the login ID (\cref{app:student-identity}).
This file is the only place where a student's start and end answers
meet, and the change score of \cref{approach-measure} is the
difference between two rows of it, so the key has to be one a student
cannot share.
A name can be shared, even here where there is only one course; the
login ID cannot, which is why the quiz program attaches it and why we
ask that program rather than read the name column ourselves.
The file also holds consenting students only: the consent lives in
the background quiz (\cref{ethics}), so the program fetches that
report first and filters both administrations through the quiz
program's own filter (\cref{app:consent-filter})---the same
functions, so the two programs cannot disagree about who consented.

The survey descriptions are looked up beside the program, as the
quiz program looks up its own: [[make programs]] writes them there,
and the answers can then be fetched from any working directory.

<<write the rspq csv>>=
SURVEY_DIRECTORY = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))


def survey_titles(which):
    """Return the item titles of the start or end survey description."""
    return analyze_quiz.item_titles(
        os.path.join(SURVEY_DIRECTORY, SURVEY_FILES[which])
    )


def write_rspq(filename):
    """Fetch both administrations and write the rspq.csv file.

    Only rows of students who consented in the background quiz are
    written.
    """
    consent = analyze_quiz.consenting(
        analyze_quiz.fetch_report(
            analyze_quiz.QUIZ_TITLES["background"],
            analyze_quiz.titles_of("background"),
        )
    )
    fieldnames = ["student", "administration"] + [
        f"item{number}" for number in range(1, 21)
    ]
    with open(filename, "w", newline="") as outfile:
        writer = csv.DictWriter(outfile, fieldnames=fieldnames)
        writer.writeheader()
        for which, title in SURVEY_TITLES.items():
            rows = analyze_quiz.filter_consent(
                analyze_quiz.fetch_report(title, survey_titles(which)),
                consent,
            )
            for row in rows:
                student = analyze_quiz.student_of(row)
                mapped = answers(row)
                if mapped is None:
                    print(
                        f"skipping incomplete answers:"
                        f" {student} ({which})",
                        file=sys.stderr,
                    )
                    continue
                writer.writerow(
                    {"student": student, "administration": which}
                    | mapped
                )
    print(f"wrote {filename}")
@

<<rspq main>>=
def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument(
        "-o",
        "--output",
        default="rspq.csv",
        help="output CSV file (default rspq.csv)",
    )
    args = parser.parse_args()
    write_rspq(args.output)
@
